- [ ] **Step 7: Write `src/api/types.ts`**

Hand-written TS interfaces mirroring the backend's Pydantic response schemas. Read the actual backend schema files to get field names/types exactly right rather than guessing — `backend/app/zones/schemas.py`, `backend/app/tasks/schemas.py`, `backend/app/goals/schemas.py`, `backend/app/violations/schemas.py`, `backend/app/pings/schemas.py`, `backend/app/auth/schemas.py`. At minimum: `Zone`, `ZoneAssignment`, `Task`, `Goal`, `GoalProgress`, `ZoneViolation`, `PushSubscription`, `LatestPing`, `User`. Use `string` for UUID and ISO-datetime fields (no runtime validation library needed for this size — Pydantic already validated server-side; the frontend just needs the shape for the compiler).

- [ ] **Step 8: Write the per-resource API modules**

Each is a thin set of typed functions calling `apiFetch`. Example shape (`src/api/pings.ts`):

```ts
import { apiFetch } from './client'
import type { LatestPing } from './types'

export function getLatestPings(): Promise<LatestPing[]> {
  return apiFetch<LatestPing[]>('/api/v1/pings/latest')
}
```

```ts
// src/api/users.ts
import { apiFetch } from './client'
import type { User } from './types'

export function getUsers(role?: string): Promise<User[]> {
  const query = role ? `?role=${encodeURIComponent(role)}` : ''
  return apiFetch<User[]>(`/api/v1/users${query}`)
}
```

Write `zones.ts`, `tasks.ts`, `goals.ts`, `violations.ts`, `pushSubscriptions.ts` analogously, covering every GET/POST/PATCH endpoint that exists today in the corresponding backend router (read each backend router file to get the exact paths/methods/request-bodies right — do not guess). This task only needs `pings.ts` and `users.ts` wired into anything (the map); the rest exist now so Phase 7b doesn't have to build the client from scratch, but aren't consumed by any component yet — that's expected, not dead code to remove.

